{"duration_api_ms":86938,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"cd72ab78-2412-465b-a3dc-383faa32d713","total_cost_usd":0.501458,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":93426,"cache_read_input_tokens":208410,"output_tokens":8606,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1593},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":93426,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2300,"cache_read_input_tokens":101566,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1400,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1400},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":6,"outputTokens":8606,"cacheReadInputTokens":208410,"cacheCreationInputTokens":93426,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.501458,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":1593,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":4,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1\",\"requirement\":\"Allow node2 (lan-a) to reach node3 (lan-b) on TCP port 5432\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"VyOS forward-filter rule 20 accepts TCP/5432 from eth1(lan-a)->eth2(lan-b). Data-plane test shows a socat listener on node3:5432 reachable from node2 via nc with successful payload echo, both before and after the mandatory restart cycle.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-fc91bf71c4b74b36\",\"cmd-c5bd8562b37d4914\",\"cmd-5434e6c03db241a3\",\"cmd-d7a3fc99ff1442b0\",\"cmd-90e10fe148bb4a47\",\"cmd-b62569871d184d1c\",\"cmd-86ab9bb6ffc043b7\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Reject all other traffic between lan-a and lan-b (other ports, ICMP, reverse direction)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"VyOS config shows default-action drop plus explicit reject rules 30 (eth1->eth2) and 40 (eth2->eth1) covering all traffic not matched by rule 20/established-related rule 10. Live tests confirm other TCP ports, ICMP between LANs, and reverse-direction TCP/5432 are rejected, reproduced consistently before and after the restart cycle.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c14f8ac336ff4fa4\",\"cmd-c5bd8562b37d4914\",\"cmd-2f9c9f2ab1724476\",\"cmd-d7a3fc99ff1442b0\",\"cmd-91fd0a51f6b1485d\",\"cmd-90e10fe148bb4a47\",\"cmd-86ab9bb6ffc043b7\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Implement using Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An Ansible inventory and playbook were authored on the controller (node4) and executed with ansible-playbook to configure node2/node3 static LAN addressing/routes and node1's VyOS interface addressing and firewall policy. A subsequent run shows fully idempotent success across all three managed hosts, and the playbook was re-run successfully after the restart cycle to reconfirm end state.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-33f52113dd9141ed\",\"cmd-dca1b805100944d7\",\"cmd-8ece7d200f9f410a\",\"cmd-0aac894c879045ed\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map directly to diagnosing and fixing the VyOS/Ansible integration (module compatibility with Python 2.7/3.5 on node2/node3, the 'configure' bash-alias-expansion problem under Ansible's raw/become wrapping, and VyOS's lack of a 'reject' default-action). Several interim mutations were bounded and fully reverted: an ad-hoc interface description was set on node1's eth1 during troubleshooting ('test' via cmd-8037e94dc74c4b10, not committed; 'testroot' committed via cmd-6a620cf2b9cd47ce; 'sudotest' committed via cmd-391a7a144eab4695) and later explicitly deleted (cmd-de098584d96b426c), leaving no residue. Ephemeral socat listeners used for connectivity testing on node2/node3 were started and killed each time, a low-risk and fully-cleaned pattern. One partially-successful playbook apply on node1 (cmd-fc91bf71c4b74b36) left invalid firewall state (state established/related as leaf values) that required an explicit delete/reset in the next apply (cmd-c14f8ac336ff4fa4) - this was corrective, not abandoned residue. The ad-hoc filesystem authorized_keys write to node1's vyos user (cmd-c6661ff3fd724179) turned out not to persist across VyOS reboot and was superseded by a persistent config-based key addition (cmd-770dbf72dcf042c3). No unnecessary broad, destructive, or security-weakening mutations were observed; failed-services and package-health checks show no residual damage after_executor.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8037e94dc74c4b10\",\"cmd-6a620cf2b9cd47ce\",\"cmd-391a7a144eab4695\",\"cmd-de098584d96b426c\",\"cmd-c14f8ac336ff4fa4\",\"cmd-c6661ff3fd724179\",\"cmd-770dbf72dcf042c3\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node4:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The Ansible-implemented VyOS firewall satisfies the task contract: node2->node3 TCP/5432 is allowed and all other cross-LAN traffic (other ports, ICMP, reverse direction) is rejected, verified via direct data-plane tests both before and after a full four-node restart cycle. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all four nodes with baseline boot_id, restart, changed boot_id, and recovered functional state each time, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots showing boot_id changes for all nodes between after_prepare and after_executor. A persistence gap in node1's ad-hoc SSH key was discovered and correctly remediated via VyOS's native persistent config path, with a final idempotent playbook re-run confirming durable end state. Minor troubleshooting mutations (interface description edits, temporary socat listeners) were bounded and reverted.\",\"limitations\":[\"The after_prepare snapshot is an alias of before_prepare (prepare phase disabled), so preparer-vs-executor attribution relies entirely on the executor command timeline rather than an independent snapshot diff.\",\"Node1's stale filesystem-based authorized_keys entry for the vyos user (added early via cmd-c6661ff3fd724179) was not explicitly removed after the persistent config-based key was added, though it appears harmless and was superseded.\",\"VyOS lacks a native 'reject' default-action, so the implementation relies on default-action drop plus explicit bidirectional reject rules; this is a reasonable interpretation of 'reject all other traffic' but differs slightly from a literal global default-reject posture.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Allow node2 (lan-a) to reach node3 (lan-b) on TCP port 5432","status":"satisfied","summary":"VyOS forward-filter rule 20 accepts TCP/5432 from eth1(lan-a)->eth2(lan-b). Data-plane test shows a socat listener on node3:5432 reachable from node2 via nc with successful payload echo, both before and after the mandatory restart cycle.","evidence_ids":["cmd-fc91bf71c4b74b36","cmd-c5bd8562b37d4914","cmd-5434e6c03db241a3","cmd-d7a3fc99ff1442b0","cmd-90e10fe148bb4a47","cmd-b62569871d184d1c","cmd-86ab9bb6ffc043b7"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Reject all other traffic between lan-a and lan-b (other ports, ICMP, reverse direction)","status":"satisfied","summary":"VyOS config shows default-action drop plus explicit reject rules 30 (eth1->eth2) and 40 (eth2->eth1) covering all traffic not matched by rule 20/established-related rule 10. Live tests confirm other TCP ports, ICMP between LANs, and reverse-direction TCP/5432 are rejected, reproduced consistently before and after the restart cycle.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c14f8ac336ff4fa4","cmd-c5bd8562b37d4914","cmd-2f9c9f2ab1724476","cmd-d7a3fc99ff1442b0","cmd-91fd0a51f6b1485d","cmd-90e10fe148bb4a47","cmd-86ab9bb6ffc043b7"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Implement using Ansible","status":"satisfied","summary":"An Ansible inventory and playbook were authored on the controller (node4) and executed with ansible-playbook to configure node2/node3 static LAN addressing/routes and node1's VyOS interface addressing and firewall policy. A subsequent run shows fully idempotent success across all three managed hosts, and the playbook was re-run successfully after the restart cycle to reconfirm end state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-33f52113dd9141ed","cmd-dca1b805100944d7","cmd-8ece7d200f9f410a","cmd-0aac894c879045ed"]}],"confidence":0.85,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.75,"summary":"Most state-changing commands map directly to diagnosing and fixing the VyOS/Ansible integration (module compatibility with Python 2.7/3.5 on node2/node3, the 'configure' bash-alias-expansion problem under Ansible's raw/become wrapping, and VyOS's lack of a 'reject' default-action). Several interim mutations were bounded and fully reverted: an ad-hoc interface description was set on node1's eth1 during troubleshooting ('test' via cmd-8037e94dc74c4b10, not committed; 'testroot' committed via cmd-6a620cf2b9cd47ce; 'sudotest' committed via cmd-391a7a144eab4695) and later explicitly deleted (cmd-de098584d96b426c), leaving no residue. Ephemeral socat listeners used for connectivity testing on node2/node3 were started and killed each time, a low-risk and fully-cleaned pattern. One partially-successful playbook apply on node1 (cmd-fc91bf71c4b74b36) left invalid firewall state (state established/related as leaf values) that required an explicit delete/reset in the next apply (cmd-c14f8ac336ff4fa4) - this was corrective, not abandoned residue. The ad-hoc filesystem authorized_keys write to node1's vyos user (cmd-c6661ff3fd724179) turned out not to persist across VyOS reboot and was superseded by a persistent config-based key addition (cmd-770dbf72dcf042c3). No unnecessary broad, destructive, or security-weakening mutations were observed; failed-services and package-health checks show no residual damage after_executor.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8037e94dc74c4b10","cmd-6a620cf2b9cd47ce","cmd-391a7a144eab4695","cmd-de098584d96b426c","cmd-c14f8ac336ff4fa4","cmd-c6661ff3fd724179","cmd-770dbf72dcf042c3","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node4:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The Ansible-implemented VyOS firewall satisfies the task contract: node2->node3 TCP/5432 is allowed and all other cross-LAN traffic (other ports, ICMP, reverse direction) is rejected, verified via direct data-plane tests both before and after a full four-node restart cycle. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all four nodes with baseline boot_id, restart, changed boot_id, and recovered functional state each time, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots showing boot_id changes for all nodes between after_prepare and after_executor. A persistence gap in node1's ad-hoc SSH key was discovered and correctly remediated via VyOS's native persistent config path, with a final idempotent playbook re-run confirming durable end state. Minor troubleshooting mutations (interface description edits, temporary socat listeners) were bounded and reverted.","limitations":["The after_prepare snapshot is an alias of before_prepare (prepare phase disabled), so preparer-vs-executor attribution relies entirely on the executor command timeline rather than an independent snapshot diff.","Node1's stale filesystem-based authorized_keys entry for the vyos user (added early via cmd-c6661ff3fd724179) was not explicitly removed after the persistent config-based key was added, though it appears harmless and was superseded.","VyOS lacks a native 'reject' default-action, so the implementation relies on default-action drop plus explicit bidirectional reject rules; this is a reasonable interpretation of 'reject all other traffic' but differs slightly from a literal global default-reject posture."]},"ttft_ms":21609,"type":"result","duration_ms":87156,"uuid":"1905a163-e8b8-43b0-869c-fc42dd5cc96c","ttft_stream_ms":1536,"time_to_request_ms":197,"first_content_frame_ms":1537,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
